/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The horizontal angle PQR at station Q is the angle between the vertical planes through QP and QR; the theodolite measures it on the horizontal circle whatever the heights of P and R.
   - To read a vernier, take the degrees and minutes at the main-scale mark just before the vernier's zero, then find the vernier line that coincides with a main-scale line and add its value.
   - In the repetition method the angle is added on the circle several times without reading in between, and the total is divided by the number of repetitions.
   - In the direction method (reiteration), several stations A, B, C, D are sighted in turn clockwise from one reference, and the readings are booked as directions; each angle is the difference of two directions.
   - A vertical angle α is the angle between the line of sight and the horizontal in a vertical plane: an angle of elevation (positive) or depression (negative).
   - zL, zR are the zenith readings on face left and face right of the same point.
   - To observe, centre the altitude bubble (or let the compensator settle), bisect the point with the horizontal hair on face left, read the vertical circle, then repeat on face right.
*/
