/* book exam traps: Averaging vernier B without dropping 180°. B reads about 180° more than A; only its minutes and seconds are averaged with A. | Dividing by n without subtracting the initial reading, or forgetting the 360° added each time the circle passes zero in repetition. | Applying the same correction to every direction after closing the horizon. The corrections grow step by step, from 0 at the reference to −e at the closing sight. | Taking a zenith reading as the vertical angle. α = 90° − z on face left; a z above 90° means a depression. | Expecting changing face to remove the error of a vertical axis that is not vertical, or refraction. Neither reverses with the face. */
